WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.010
Adam Plantenburg: Nice Owen Garitty: Nice
Adam: You want me to smile at the camera?

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:17.010
Stoic?

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.010
Just chill?

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:23.000
Owen: Are you going to do the [makes a serious
face] Hold on, should I have my tea?

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Probably.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.160
I’ve been sitting here for 15 minutes trying
to figure out how to intro this video, so…here’s

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:32.200
the intro to the video…I’m Owen with FPW
Media.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.160
Adam: I’m Adam from FPW Media.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.579
Owen: And we’re here to talk about a video
we recently did called I AM APEX for Timber

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:44.710
Products Company specifically for their Ampine
division, specifically when they came to us

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:50.989
about launching their new product Apex, which
is a composite panel product.

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:57.019
We really worked hard and thought about how
can we make that visually interesting?

00:00:57.019 --> 00:01:04.199
We decided to have the story of the production
of one of these panels be told from the perspective

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:05.199
of the panel.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:07.890
So we shot the whole project on a Red camera.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.570
We shot on a Red Epic W and a Red Epic Dragon.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:15.440
What is really interesting is that the mills
are really, really, really dark.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:16.960
Like…you can attest to this…

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:17.960
Adam Plantenburg: Insanely dark!

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.330
Owen Garitty: Insanely dark.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:22.770
So we shot most of it at 3200 ISO (I think)?

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:23.770
Adam: Yeah.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:24.770
Owen: Is that right?

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:25.770
That seems about right, yeah.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:26.810
Which is nuts.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.530
And I mean, it looked fantastic.

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:35.110
We shot it mostly on Canon still glass L series
lenses, with a cinema lens here and there

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:36.110
thrown in.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:39.430
I’d say it’s an artistic decision but
it was also driven by necessity but it was

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:44.810
really awesome how it worked out in that the
lighting…we knew that going in lighting

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.510
was going to be a huge problem.

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:53.690
We’ve shot in a lot of mills before so we
decided to embrace that lighting was going

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:58.820
to be a challenge and actually psychically
show a lot of our lights in the shots because

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.570
in the mills there are all sorts of different
lights that are different bulbs, different

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:08.170
color temperatures, and we knew that we were
going to have no control over that.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:12.599
Adam: Yeah, because a lot of the times it
looked like what we’re shooting is a spot

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:17.341
that needs to be examined anyways, so it could
be a light that’s spotted on there for a

00:02:17.341 --> 00:02:18.341
specific reason.

00:02:18.341 --> 00:02:19.341
Owen: Right.

00:02:19.341 --> 00:02:24.090
So probably one of the hardest shots (I would
say from a safety perspective and just like

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:30.420
a planning perspective) was our shots in the
furnished room where the dust comes in and

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:35.730
the furnish comes into be made into the board
and the graded footage (you can’t really

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:41.250
tell) but there it was so dusty that when
they were moving, it was probably like a whiteout

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.250
almost.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:46.730
I mean, Adam was basically…and I don’t
actually know, were you behind me?

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:51.560
Adam: Yeah, so I had one hand on your safety
vest just kind of pulling you around, making

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.520
sure that you didn’t run into anything.

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:59.100
That if the loader was coming at you, we weren’t
too close and then at one point your safety

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.620
glasses fell off and you must have just been
filming blind essentially.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:03.620
Owen: I basically was.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:07.560
I mean it was like I kind of knew where I
could see where the loader was and I could

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:11.721
kind of see the camera, but I was just imaging
the framing because I couldn’t even see

00:03:11.721 --> 00:03:16.930
the screen because the screen got completely
covered over with dust at one point, but it

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.290
turned out really well.

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:23.510
Adam: And as soon as the take was over it
would be us running out putting the camera

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.209
on the stand and then just blowing it off
like crazy…

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:27.550
Owen: Oh, no!

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:29.349
Where did Adam go?

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:35.520
I’m sitting here with Jake, our senior editor
at FPW Media to talk about the post-production

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.270
process on I AM APEX.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:42.630
Jake do you want to kind of take us through
our last product shot where we get back into

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:43.630
the narrative?

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:47.390
Jake Sullivan: Yeah, and sometimes that’s
great because that one was actually really

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:48.390
easy.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:54.630
That was kind of borne out of the camera movement
and then all I had to do was really dip to

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:59.819
black there and pay close attention to where
we were making the actual cut in both of those.

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:03.180
Owen: And we kind of threw you a curveball
in that one a little bit because that part

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:09.690
of the project wasn’t shot until almost
the whole edit was done, I would say so you

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:16.090
had this kind of gaping hole for most of the
edit where those product shots were supposedly

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.090
going to go.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:21.280
Jake: And those were also kind of fun because
we had a lot of visual effects to cover things

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:22.840
that we couldn’t get in camera.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:24.970
Owen: So you added more fire, what else?

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:30.240
Jake: More fire in the studio is kind of hard
to not light things on fire [laughs]…so…

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.350
Owen: Right.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:35.920
And then on the moisture scene, you added
a couple layers in front?

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:36.920
Or how did that work?

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:40.840
Jake: I added a couple layers of smoke in
front and then I masked out the actual piece

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:46.130
and added a couple behind it to give that
sense of depth that it has.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:49.900
Owen: All and all, I think it is one of the
projects that I’m most proud of.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.090
We had a blast filming it and if anybody has
any questions about the production or the

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:58.810
gear we used or anything like that, please
reach out to us and make sure to watch the

00:04:58.810 --> 00:04:59.810
video in full.

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:00.370
Thanks for watching!

